cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "SORTEE 2026 Hackathon: Data & Code Assessment FAQ"
message: >-
  If you use this guide, please cite it using the metadata in this file. If you
  use the Dragon Kill Points approach to credit contributions, please also cite
  Martinig et al. (2026), listed under `references` below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Edward
    name-particle: R.
    family-names: Ivimey-Cook
    email: e.ivimeycook@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4910-0443"
repository-code: "https://github.com/EIvimeyCook/Data-Code_Society"
url: "https://eivimeycook.github.io/Data-Code_Society/"
abstract: >-
  A question-by-question FAQ for the data and code assessment form used at the
  SORTEE 2026 conference hackathon. Covers how to judge whether a paper's data
  and code are archived, accessible, licensed and documented, and how
  contributions are credited using Dragon Kill Points.
keywords:
  - open data
  - open code
  - data sharing
  - code sharing
  - reproducibility
  - research transparency
  - hackathon
  - SORTEE
